WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.325
Two-phase commit left one weak spot: a single coordinator.

00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:12.650
Replace it by keeping copies of the data on several machines.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:17.975
But copies only help if they agree, and getting every write onto them, in sync, is the whole problem of this page.

00:00:17.975 --> 00:00:21.703
Reads are easy, ask any one copy.

00:00:21.703 --> 00:00:25.432
Writes are the hard part, and option one is the obvious move: send it to all three copies, and wait for every one to confirm.

00:00:25.432 --> 00:00:29.160
Watch it break.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.889
Two replicas acknowledge fast.

00:00:32.889 --> 00:00:36.617
But the third is slow, or crashed.

00:00:36.617 --> 00:00:40.346
Since every copy must confirm, the whole write blocks.

00:00:40.346 --> 00:00:44.074
One dead replica stalls everything, so writing to all has no fault tolerance.

00:00:44.074 --> 00:00:47.563
Option two fixes it.

00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:51.051
Instead of writing to all, elect one leader and route every write through it.

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:54.540
Two wins.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.029
A write is safe once a majority confirms, not all, so a minority can fail.

00:00:58.029 --> 00:01:01.518
And one leader orders every write, so there is no vote per write.

00:01:01.518 --> 00:01:05.007
Without a leader, each write needs its own majority round, about two hundred milliseconds.

00:01:05.007 --> 00:01:08.495
With a leader, fifty.

00:01:08.495 --> 00:01:11.984
One slow vote buys a million fast writes.

00:01:11.984 --> 00:01:15.295
How does one vote pick exactly one leader?

00:01:15.295 --> 00:01:18.605
The majority.

00:01:18.605 --> 00:01:21.916
A candidate needs more than half the machines, three of five, four of seven.

00:01:21.916 --> 00:01:25.226
And any two majorities must overlap on at least one machine.

00:01:25.226 --> 00:01:28.537
That shared machine votes once, so it can never put two candidates over the top.

00:01:28.537 --> 00:01:31.847
One winner, never two.

00:01:31.847 --> 00:01:35.158
That is what rules out split-brain.

00:01:35.158 --> 00:01:39.133
So what happens when the leader dies?

00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:43.108
In steady state, one leader serves and the followers replicate its log.

00:01:43.108 --> 00:01:47.083
Then the leader fails, and its heartbeats stop.

00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:51.057
The first follower to time out becomes a candidate and asks the others for votes.

00:01:51.057 --> 00:01:55.032
Does it lead yet?

00:01:55.032 --> 00:01:59.007
It wins a majority, takes over, and resumes heartbeats, about two hundred milliseconds after the crash.

00:01:59.007 --> 00:02:02.982
The election runs only on failure, not as a routine of stages.

00:02:02.982 --> 00:02:07.347
The leader now takes every write, and it has to get each one onto the followers.

00:02:07.347 --> 00:02:11.713
When does it tell the client a write is safe?

00:02:11.713 --> 00:02:16.078
Synchronous replication waits for a follower to acknowledge before it replies okay.

00:02:16.078 --> 00:02:20.443
Nothing is lost on a failover, but every write pays a full round trip.

00:02:20.443 --> 00:02:24.808
Asynchronous replication replies okay immediately and ships the write to the followers later.

00:02:24.808 --> 00:02:29.174
That is fast.

00:02:29.174 --> 00:02:33.539
But if the leader crashes, the writes that had not shipped yet are gone.

00:02:33.539 --> 00:02:38.118
Raft elects one leader by a majority, so thousands of machines act as one, even as machines die.

00:02:38.118 --> 00:02:42.697
The leader then replicates to its followers, synchronously for zero loss, or asynchronously for speed.

00:02:42.697 --> 00:02:47.276
But all of it leans on the network.

00:02:47.276 --> 00:02:51.855
When the network splits, you cannot always reach a majority or a fresh copy.

00:02:51.855 --> 00:02:56.434
That tradeoff is next.
